        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            61.2,
            57.27,
            186.0,
            74.17
          ],
          "blockIndex": 1,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "## the six steps"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            97.39,
            499.0,
            123.26
          ],
          "blockIndex": 2,
          "blockType": "project_boundary_heading",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "STEP 01\nSkip the theory at first. Get a breadboard and an Arduino kit."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            126.9,
            540.4,
            163.63
          ],
          "blockIndex": 3,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "DO THIS\nBuy a starter kit, not individual parts. A $30–40 Arduino Uno clone kit gives you the\nboard, a breadboard, jumper wires, a resistor assortment, LEDs, buttons, a buzzer, a"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            165.47,
            430.0,
            177.43
          ],
          "blockIndex": 4,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "potentiometer and a few sensors — everything for the first month."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            183.3,
            540.4,
            220.03
          ],
          "blockIndex": 5,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "THE TRAP\nBuying a 'proper' bench setup before you know what you'll use. A $200 oscilloscope on\nday one teaches you nothing; a $2 LED does."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            225.9,
            540.4,
            262.63
          ],
          "blockIndex": 6,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "YOU'RE DONE WHEN\nThe kit is on your desk and the Arduino IDE (or PlatformIO) is installed and sees the\nboard."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            292.49,
            367.9,
            318.36
          ],
          "blockIndex": 7,
          "blockType": "project_boundary_heading",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "STEP 02\nBlink an LED. Then a buzzer. Then a button."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            322.0,
            529.36,
            344.93
          ],
          "blockIndex": 8,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "DO THIS\nUpload the Blink example, then change the delay from 1000 ms to 100 ms and watch it"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            346.77,
            523.84,
            372.53
          ],
          "blockIndex": 9,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "change. Wire an LED with a 220 ohm resistor on pin 13. Add the buzzer with tone().\nAdd a button with pinMode(INPUT_PULLUP) so the LED only lights while it's pressed."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            378.4,
            523.84,
            415.13
          ],
          "blockIndex": 10,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "THE TRAP\nCopy-pasting a whole project on day two. Every build here is under 15 lines — type\nthem, break them, fix them."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            421.0,
            496.24,
            457.73
          ],
          "blockIndex": 11,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "YOU'RE DONE WHEN\nYou can make the LED, the buzzer and the button work together without looking\nanything up."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            487.59,
            312.7,
            513.46
          ],
          "blockIndex": 12,
          "blockType": "project_boundary_heading",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "STEP 03\nNow learn the theory you just used."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            517.1,
            534.88,
            567.63
          ],
          "blockIndex": 13,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "DO THIS\nYou've already used Ohm's law (that 220 ohm resistor), Kirchhoff's laws (the current\nthrough your LED loop) and a pull-up resistor. Go back and read exactly those: V =\nIR, series vs parallel, voltage dividers, why the LED needs a resistor at all. The"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            569.47,
            319.6,
            581.43
          ],
          "blockIndex": 14,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "concepts stick because you've held the parts."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            587.3,
            534.88,
            610.23
          ],
          "blockIndex": 15,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "THE TRAP\nTrying to finish a 900-page textbook before touching a wire. Read one chapter, build"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            612.07,
            170.56,
            624.03
          ],
          "blockIndex": 16,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "one thing, repeat."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            629.9,
            518.32,
            652.83
          ],
          "blockIndex": 17,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "YOU'RE DONE WHEN\nYou can calculate the resistor for any LED from its datasheet and explain why 5 V"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            654.67,
            236.8,
            666.63
          ],
          "blockIndex": 18,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 2,
          "text": "straight into an LED kills it."
        }
      ],
      "charCount": 1719,
      "height": 792.0,
      "pageNumber": 2,
      "textExtractable": true,
      "width": 612.0
    },
    {
