            95.37,
            534.88,
            107.33
          ],
          "blockIndex": 2,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Arduino Uno clone starter kit (board, breadboard, wires, LEDs, resistors, buttons,"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            109.17,
            330.64,
            134.93
          ],
          "blockIndex": 3,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "buzzer, pot, sensors) — $30–40\n• Digital multimeter with continuity beep — $15"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            136.77,
            446.56,
            148.73
          ],
          "blockIndex": 4,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Temperature-controlled soldering iron kit + solder + flux — $20–25"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            150.57,
            374.8,
            176.33
          ],
          "blockIndex": 5,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Through-hole soldering practice kit — $5\n• USB cable and a phone charger as your 5 V supply — $0"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            178.17,
            529.36,
            203.93
          ],
          "blockIndex": 6,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Later, not now: ESP32 board ($6) once you want Wi-Fi, a basic oscilloscope ($60+)\nonce you care about signals"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            61.2,
            231.68,
            326.4,
            248.57
          ],
          "blockIndex": 7,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "## the first five builds, in order"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            269.77,
            507.28,
            295.53
          ],
          "blockIndex": 8,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Blink — change the timing, then blink two LEDs alternately\n• Button-controlled LED — learn INPUT_PULLUP and why the button reads backwards"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            297.37,
            424.48,
            323.13
          ],
          "blockIndex": 9,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Buzzer melody — tone() and a short array of notes\n• Potentiometer dimmer — analogRead in, PWM out with analogWrite"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            324.97,
            540.4,
            350.73
          ],
          "blockIndex": 10,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Light-sensor night light — a photoresistor in a voltage divider turns the LED on in\nthe dark"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            61.2,
            378.47,
            381.0,
            395.38
          ],
          "blockIndex": 11,
          "blockType": "paragraph",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "## the only theory you need for month one"
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            416.57,
            391.36,
            428.53
          ],
          "blockIndex": 12,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Ohm's law, V = IR: picks every resistor you'll ever add."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            430.37,
            518.32,
            469.93
          ],
          "blockIndex": 13,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Kirchhoff's current and voltage laws: what goes in comes out; voltages around a\nloop add to zero. That's how you reason about any circuit.\n• Series vs parallel: series shares current, parallel shares voltage."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            471.77,
            507.28,
            497.53
          ],
          "blockIndex": 14,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Voltage divider: two resistors turn any sensor into a readable voltage.\n• LED current limiting: (5 V - 2 V) / 220 ohm is about 14 mA — bright and safe."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            499.37,
            485.2,
            511.33
          ],
          "blockIndex": 15,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• Pull-up and pull-down resistors: why a floating input reads random noise."
        },
        {
          "bbox": [
            71.2,
            513.17,
            446.56,
            525.13
          ],
          "blockIndex": 16,
          "blockType": "list",
          "confidence": "EXACT",
          "pageNumber": 4,
          "text": "• PWM: switching fast enough that a dimmer looks smooth to your eye."
        }
      ],
